Some ideas run through the whole project. Others belong to one module and are
explained where that module lives. This page gathers the project-wide ones, the
vocabulary and the contracts you carry into every layer.

## Capabilities, CapTP, and OCapN

A capability is just a reference you cannot fake. If you hold one, you can call the
object it points at, and if you do not, there is no name to guess and no flag to
flip. Authority travels by passing the reference itself, which is why the three
moves that matter all fall out for free. You delegate by handing the reference along,
you attenuate by handing along a narrower wrapper instead, and you revoke by dropping
the wrapper so the calls stop landing.

[A capability is a reference, not a copy. Pass it as an argument and the partner can call it; if you never receive it, there is nothing to forge.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

That picture is static. It says who is allowed to call whom. A call has a shape of
its own. You send it and hold a promise the same instant, without waiting for the
partner to do the work, and the partner's later resolve settles that promise into a
result.

[A single call, start to finish: you deliver it and immediately hold a promise, and the partner's resolve settles that promise to a result.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-call.svg'))

If this is new, start at the beginning.
[Capabilities, CapTP, and OCapN, from zero](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/capabilities.md)
explains what a capability is and why this beats ambient authority, and what
[CapTP](http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/) adds over a plain socket, an idea
that traces back to the [E language](http://erights.org/), all before any pancake API
appears. It also introduces [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/)
and the [OCapN community](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn), which together are the
conformance authority everything here is measured against.

## Locators: reaching a peer in the first place

A capability is a reference you already hold, which leaves one fair question: how do
you get the *first* one, from a peer you have never met? You cannot forge a
reference, so that is not the way in. The way in is a **locator**, and locators are
a pillar of OCapN in their own right, alongside the wire protocol and the value
model.

A locator is an *address*, not a reference, and it is exactly what an `ocapn://` URI
encodes. The URI is the locator written down: the text you paste into a config, store,
or share out of band. Parse it and you get the structured locator back, so the two are
one address in two forms. It carries three parts: a transport (`tcp`, `iroh`), a
designator that names the peer on that transport, and any hints needed to connect,
spelled `ocapn://<designator>.<transport>`. You bring a locator in out of band; from it
you open a session, and every peer offers one *bootstrap object* you can fetch a
published capability from. That fetched capability is your first live reference, and from
there you are back in capability-land, where references travel by being passed and no one
needs a locator again.

A locator can also carry **hints**: `?key=value` pairs tacked onto the end of the URI,
as in `ocapn://<node-id>.iroh?…`. Hints are how a transport learns the extra it needs
to reach a peer when the designator alone will not do. iroh leans on them: a node id
is self-authenticating but not routable without hints, so dialing needs explicit
direct-address hints in the query. They are plain text on the wire and carry no type
system, only `key=value` strings. And they are only ever a routing aid, never
authority: a wrong or missing hint can stop you from *reaching* a peer, but no hint can
let you *forge* one.

A **sturdyref** is the long-lived form: a locator plus a *swiss number*, an unguessable
token naming one specific object at that peer. It is offline authority: it carries
no live session state and survives a restart. Bringing it back to life is the same
two moves as any first contact: open a session per the locator, then fetch the
swiss number. What returns is an ordinary import, and the sturdyref origin disappears.
The [protocol cheatsheet]($link.page('protocol')) names the two locator records, the
[Transports]($link.page('transports')) page covers the transports a locator can
name, and the
[sturdyref and locator diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/07-sturdyrefs-and-locators.md)
draw the enlivenment flow.

> **Swiss number** — the unguessable secret a sturdyref carries to name one specific
> object. There is no list to check and no login: holding the string *is* the
> permission, and not holding it leaves nothing to guess. The name comes from the
> numbered Swiss bank account, where the number alone is the access.

## The conversations on the wire

[The Spritely Goblins mascot, sizing up a stack of pancakes. pancake is checked against a real Goblins peer, so the two have to agree on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('pancake-goblin-512.png'))

A protocol is easiest to trust when you can see it. The
[protocol diagrams](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/README.md)
draw the CapTP conversations pancake is built to honor: the session lifecycle,
deliver and resolve, promises and pipelining, references and tables, garbage
collection, third-party handoffs, and sturdyrefs. They are the picture the code is
checked against.

> **Promise pipelining** — when you call a remote object you get a *promise* for the
> answer right away, and you may call methods on that promise before it has resolved.
> Each follow-on call is forwarded to wherever the answer turns out to live, so a
> chain of dependent calls makes one round trip instead of one per step.

> **Three-party handoff** — the move that lets one peer introduce you to another. If a
> peer you trust holds a reference to a third party and wants to pass it to you, CapTP
> carries a signed introduction so you can present it to that third party yourself and
> call them directly, rather than relaying every call through the peer in the middle.

### Codecs: the seam beneath the conversation

Those messages still have to be written down as bytes, and OCapN settles how: the
format is Syrup, and pancake speaks Syrup wherever the protocol calls for it. But
Syrup is just a *codec*, one way to write a message down, **not the protocol itself**,
and pancake keeps the two apart. The session deals in messages, a deliver or a
resolve or a promise, and never in bytes; a separate codec layer turns those
messages into bytes and back, and the session never learns which codec did the work.

That separation is what lets pancake carry a second codec, Cap'n Proto beside Syrup,
and even one you bring yourself. The [Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) page lays out
which codecs ship, how they trade off, and how to add another.

### CapTP versions, and who is on the other end

Picking a codec is only half of it. Two peers also settle on a *version* of the
protocol itself, and the way they settle it is negotiation, not luck. Each peer brings a
small **menu** to the handshake: the CapTP versions it can speak, the codecs it can
speak, and the optional features it supports, each listed best-first. Both ends then run
the *same* rule over the two menus: keep the version, codec, and features the two
share, most-preferred first. The rule is symmetric, so both reach the identical pick,
with nothing to haggle over and no extra round trip.

The one thing each side needs is to *see* the other's menu before it commits. When the
peers share a process that is free: both menus are already in hand. Over a network, the
partner's menu rides along in **hints** on its `ocapn://` locator (such as the
`pancake/codec` hint), so by the time a session opens each side already knows what the
other can do.

[Both peers run the same rule over the two menus and reach the same pick.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-negotiation.svg'))

Only once that pick is made does `op:start-session` go on the wire, carrying the agreed
version string, `1.0` or `pancake-pre-spec`. The two strings line up because both sides
computed the same answer, not because they happened to match. And if the menus genuinely
share no version or no codec, there is no overlap to choose, so the session aborts
rather than guess.

That is why pancake ends up speaking two dialects, depending on who answers.

[The dialect depends on who answers: the canonical floor against Goblins, pancake's own profile between two pancake peers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-dialects.svg'))

Against [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/), the shared menu is just
the floor: CapTP **1.0** over Syrup, exactly what the canonical implementation speaks.
That is the interop anchor: if pancake and Goblins disagree, pancake is the one that is
wrong. Against another **pancake** peer, the shared menu reaches higher: a
pancake-native profile it labels `pancake-pre-spec`, and the Cap'n Proto codec, with
1.0-over-Syrup still on it as common ground. The application code is identical across
both; only the bytes and the version label on the wire change. The
[session lifecycle diagram](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/01-session-lifecycle.md)
draws the handshake and the abort a no-overlap negotiation produces.

## Frames, ownership, and the memory contract

pancake treats every allocation and copy on the wire path as a design event. Each one
is forbidden, named, or measured, and never left to chance. The rule that makes this
tractable is an explicit split in who owns the bytes. A `Frame` owns its wire bytes, a
`FrameView` borrows them for a bounded scope, and session state never holds on to
borrowed memory. The budget that falls out is two numbers. A steady-state delivery to a
live export costs exactly **one heap allocation**: the owned-frame copy the event model
requires, and nothing more. And a whole idle connection holds **under about two
kilobytes** of protocol state. A build gate holds both, so they stay facts rather than
hopes. The
[architecture](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/architecture.md)
holds the full memory and copy contract.

## Built for half a million connections on one node

That budget is not abstract tidiness. It is what one target demands. pancake is built
for a single ordinary server to hold about **500,000** live OCapN connections at once.
At that scale memory gives out long before CPU does, so the per-connection numbers above
are the whole game: two kilobytes across 500k is well under a gigabyte of core state.

Half a million is a design target, not a count clocked on a live wire. It is large enough
to force a sans-io core, a shared read-buffer pool, and sub-two-kilobyte sessions, and small
enough to fit on one tuned machine if the per-connection memory budget holds. Above that
scale the limit is the kernel's — socket memory, file descriptors, syscall cost — and
pancake has not measured those yet. Until it has, 500k is the largest fan-out paired with
a budget the build gate already defends.

The [ladder](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/docs/ladder.md) has the
exact ceilings and the arithmetic; the [Performance]($link.page('performance')) page has
the benchmarks and how to run them.

## What proves it

Every semantic claim in these docs is backed by something that runs. Three proof layers do
the work:

- **Live Goblins interop.** pancake holds a real, two-way OCapN conversation with a
  [Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) peer, Goblins calling pancake
  and pancake calling back, from every binding and not just from Zig, `zig build
  goblins-interop`, an opt-in step needing Racket + Goblins on PATH.
- **The upstream [OCapN test suite](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn-test-suite).** Run with
  `zig build ocapn-conformance` against a pancake peer (opt-in; needs the suite via `uv`).
- **Cross-codec replay.** The same conversation runs through Syrup and Cap'n Proto and
  must produce an identical trace, so switching codecs cannot quietly change behavior.
  This one runs in the hermetic `zig build check`.

The [project page]($link.page('project')) tracks how much of the protocol these cover
today, and the [examples]($link.page('examples')) let you watch a round-trip run.
